### Business Logo

Use the supplied business logo when one is available.
For this theme, build the identity around a clean `ForgeCart Studio` wordmark with a simple forge-spark, storefront frame, and modular block supporting mark that can work in header, footer, favicon, Shopify/WordPress service badges, and compact interface placements.

When no logo is supplied, create an original text-based wordmark and a simple supporting mark that can be implemented as a local, accessible SVG.

The logo must remain clear in the header, footer, mobile navigation, favicon, and compact interface placements.

The logo must not copy or imitate an existing company, agency, product, or trademarked visual identity.

### Business Slogan

Use the supplied slogan when one is available.
For this theme, use the slogan "WordPress sites and Shopify stores built to sell clearly."

When no slogan is supplied, create one concise and original slogan that communicates the business value clearly without making unverifiable claims.

### Business Field

The theme must support a professional service-based business.
For this theme, the business is a web design and commerce development studio focused on modern WordPress websites, Shopify storefronts, WooCommerce builds, platform migrations, conversion-focused design systems, launch support, and ongoing site care for small businesses, service brands, creators, and growing ecommerce teams.

The generated content should present ForgeCart Studio as a practical partner for strategy, WordPress implementation, Shopify theme customization, product/catalog structure, checkout readiness, analytics handoff, content editing workflows, launch planning, and post-launch support. Keep the positioning unified: WordPress for content-rich service and publishing sites, Shopify for commerce operations, and thoughtful integration when a business needs both.

The theme must work for both consumer-facing and business-to-business services without making the site feel divided or inconsistent.


### Platform-Specific Direction

ForgeCart Studio must feel like one coherent WordPress and Shopify website agency, not two disconnected businesses. The site should clearly explain when WordPress is the right fit, when Shopify is the right fit, and how the studio supports businesses that need content, commerce, email capture, analytics, and launch operations working together.

Required service emphasis:

- Custom WordPress websites for service businesses, publishers, local brands, consultants, and content-heavy companies.
- Shopify storefront setup, theme customization, product collection architecture, merchandising sections, and checkout-readiness support.
- WooCommerce and Shopify decision guidance without claiming either platform is universally better.
- Migration planning from old WordPress, Squarespace, Wix, custom sites, or legacy Shopify themes.
- Conversion-focused UX, product/story pages, landing pages, forms, newsletter capture, analytics events, and post-launch care.
- Practical maintenance plans for updates, backups, performance checks, small content edits, seasonal campaign pages, and ecommerce tune-ups.

Do not invent real Shopify Plus status, agency partner badges, official certifications, app partnerships, client revenue, platform guarantees, uptime claims, or unsupported performance metrics.

## 02. Style / CSS Requirements

Organize the theme like a serious, modern WordPress product rather than a quick demonstration. The codebase must use predictable folders, reusable template parts, consistent SCSS architecture, shared design tokens, and component-level styling.

Do not scatter one-off CSS rules across PHP templates. Do not place large style blocks directly inside template files. Do not use inline styles unless a value must be generated dynamically and cannot be represented through a class or CSS custom property.

